  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 206 PM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nA cooler and wetter pattern is in store through the rest of the work \nweek into early this weekend. Moist southerly to southwesterly flow \naloft from Tropical Storm Polo, along with inverted surface \ntroughing brings favorable moisture and lift for daily scattered \nthunderstorm development across much of the region. The most \nfavorable areas in receiving the heaviest rainfall will be across \nportions of southeast New Mexico and far west Texas. Coverage of \nshowers and storms begin to significantly increase (> 60%) by \nWednesday for these areas as upper-level troughing pulls in more \nmoisture from northwest Mexico. Ensembles continue to signal PWATs \nreaching above the 90th climatological percentile through at least \nFriday, reaffirming the threat of heavy rainfall and flash flooding. \nRainfall amounts are still uncertain, however, probabilistic \nguidance has a low to medium (30-50%) chance for rainfall exceeding \nan inch within 24 hours (Wednesday-Thursday) for areas across the \nEddy County plains into the Guadalupe Mountains. Locally higher \namounts (4 inches or greater) due to repeated convective bands look \nto occur as well. Therefore, a Flood Watch has been issued for these \nareas from Tuesday afternoon through Thursday morning. The Watch \nwill likely need to be extended as the plume of moisture shifts east \nThursday and Friday. Areas elsewhere should still expect isolated to \nscattered rain/storms each afternoon and evening (20-50%). Localized \nflash flooding could occur outside the watch area, though less \nextensive rain coverage is expected. We will continue to monitor \ntrends closely. Otherwise, cooler temperatures will persist where \nhighs range from the 80s to low 90s for most locations. The flow \naloft becomes more zonal by the weekend, with weak ridging building \noverhead on Sunday. This should allow for lower chances of showers \nand thunderstorms this weekend, while temperatures also trend warmer \nback into the upper 80s to lower 90s over much of the region. \n\nLamberson",
